  "body": "The spec has a paragraph in it that says:\r\n\r\n> The term \"JavaScript\" is used to refer to ECMA-262, rather than the official term ECMAScript, since the term JavaScript is more widely known. Similarly, the MIME type used to refer to JavaScript in this specification is text/javascript, since that is the most commonly used type, despite it being an officially obsoleted type according to RFC 4329. [RFC4329]\r\n\r\nThis paragraph is unfortunately nowhere near section [4.12.1.2](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/scripting.html#scriptingLanguages), which is where the scripting languages and the correct mime types are described. This leaves the reader unaware that this exception exists, causing confusion when comparing this text to the [IANA spec](https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/media-types.xhtml#text), which happened to me today as I worked on updating MDN's documentation about MIME types.\r\n\r\nThis text should be located in—or duplicated into—section 4.12.1.2 in order to alleviate potential confusion.",
